$DIR/main.p:54:2: p holds a view of h (h.l.head, line 52), which was emptied by take(&h.l) at line 53, so p can no longer be used here
    p looks at memory that h owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    h.l.head at line 52. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used
    only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 53 h
    was emptied by take(&h.l), so the view may look at memory that was released,
    or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 53, or empty h only after the last use of p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
